%\VignetteIndexEntry{CCC tests and trend diagnostics}
%\VignetteEngine{utils::Sweave}
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{Sweave}
\begin{document}
\title{Common Causality of Covariates: a worked example}
\maketitle
This package implements Sunny Karim's CCC procedure. It tests restrictions on
untreated covariate coefficients, not parallel trends. HC1 is the default to
match the supplied simulation code; it is not cluster robust.
<<setup>>=
library(ccctest)
d <- read.csv(system.file("extdata","original.csv",package="ccctest"))
fit <- ccctest(y_state ~ x, d, "group", "year", "gvar")
print(fit)
@
The region-only Holm rejection selects the region specification. Selection is
not proof of the maintained model. Both auxiliary fits retain cell intercepts.
<<trends>>=
tr <- ccc_trends(y_state ~ x, d, "group", "year", "gvar")
head(tr$series)
head(tr$pairs[, c("pred_st","pred_sb","pred_ct","pred_cb")])
@
All four predictions in a comparison use the treated target-period empirical
covariate distribution. The supplied design has only two common preperiods,
so the integrated panel contains one change. Plot with \verb|plot(tr, panels="all")|.
For a richer covariate basis use \verb|y_state ~ x + I(x^2) + factor(category)|.
The package integrates means of basis columns, not transformations of means.
No observation weights, cluster inference, survey design, ATT estimator or
confidence bands are provided. A singular requested hypothesis is undetermined.
Installation: \verb|R CMD INSTALL r|. Uninstallation: \verb|remove.packages("ccctest")|.
\end{document}
